development.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-3.3,xmax=3,ymin=-1,ymax=12,xtick={-3,-2,...,2},ytick={0,2,...,12},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,red!70!black,dashed,domain=-3.3:3] {3};
\node[above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:-2,3) {$y=3$};
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-3.3:3.1,samples=100] {exp(x*ln(2))+3};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,4)};
\node[above left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0,4) {$(0,4)$};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The $+3$ raises the whole curve, so the asymptote is $y=3$.
At $x=0$, $y=2^0+3=1+3=4$, so the $y$-intercept is $(0,4)$.
